/* The handoff's primitives, as element defaults and a small class vocabulary.
 *
 * APPLIED AT THE PANEL ROOT (Shell.uxml), so a screen gets the register for
 * free and its own stylesheet only has to describe its own layout. That is
 * the opposite of the previous arrangement, where 18 stylesheets each
 * re-specified nothing and the app rendered as untinted boxes on black.
 *
 * FOUR THINGS THE HANDOFF ASKS FOR THAT USS CANNOT EXPRESS, and what is
 * done instead. These are deliberate translations, not omissions - read
 * before "fixing" one of them back to the README's literal value.
 *
 * 1. `linear-gradient(180deg, #8878cf, #6f5fbb)` on the primary CTA. SOLVED
 *    in Phase 8 with a 2x64 ramp texture at Assets/UI/Art/cta-ramp.png. The
 *    Phase 7 note said a gradient "would mean shipping a 1xN texture per CTA
 *    colour, which is real art for a difference of six hex steps" - true when
 *    it would have been the only texture in the build, and false once the
 *    icon sheet ships. One ramp, one CTA colour, no art.
 *
 * 2. `box-shadow`. USS still has none. SOLVED as a nine-sliced sprite at
 *    Assets/UI/Art/shadow-card.png, tinted by --elev-1-tint / --elev-2-tint
 *    to the handoff's two elevations. On a light theme with white on
 *    near-white, this is most of what separates a card from a rectangle.
 *
 *    .elev-1 / .elev-2 GO ON A WRAPPER, NOT ON THE CARD, and that is forced
 *    rather than chosen: UI Toolkit clips background-image to the element's
 *    own box, so a drop shadow cannot be drawn on the card it belongs to -
 *    it has to be drawn by something larger. Each class is padding plus the
 *    sprite; the card goes inside as a child and its own opaque --surface
 *    covers the middle, leaving the falloff in the padding. Measured on the
 *    Primitives fixture, putting .elev-1 on the card itself instead paints a
 *    grey smudge across its interior, because a nine-slice stretches its
 *    centre region over the whole element.
 *
 *    --elev-1-spread / --elev-2-spread ARE that padding, which is also why
 *    -unity-slice-scale differs between the two classes. Nine-slice maps
 *    texture offset p to p*scale screen px in from the element edge, and the
 *    card edge sits at exactly the padding, so scale is padding/10 - the
 *    texture carries its ink in a 10px frame. One scale cannot serve both
 *    spreads. generate-textures.py has the arithmetic.
 *
 * 3. The physical button press, `box-shadow: 0 3px 0 #5b4d9e` collapsing to
 *    `0 1px 0` with `translateY(2px)`. bible 10.6 names this specifically and
 *    says to preserve it, so it is reproduced with the properties USS does
 *    have: a 3px bottom border in `--violet-shadow` that drops to 1px while
 *    the element translates 2px down. Same silhouette, same motion, same
 *    feel under a thumb.
 *
 * 4. `border-radius: 999px` FOR A PILL. The handoff's radius table says
 *    `pill 999px` and its own markup writes it on every chip, every nav tab
 *    and all 29 of its progress tracks. In CSS that is the standard idiom
 *    for "cap the ends", because the spec scales an over-large radius down
 *    by ONE COMMON FACTOR until the corners fit - so 999px on a 99x27 box
 *    becomes a 13.5px semicircle. UI TOOLKIT CLAMPS EACH AXIS SEPARATELY
 *    INSTEAD, to half the width and half the height, and draws the ellipse
 *    quadrant that results: 49.5 wide by 13.5 tall on that same box. The
 *    end is a long shallow lens and the "pill" loses most of its straight
 *    run.
 *
 *    MEASURED, 50 rendered bars, not inferred from two examples. At a
 *    radius of exactly half the height the cap is a true semicircle at
 *    every height tested (6, 8, 21, 27, 54) and at every width tested (40
 *    to 300) - the cap length does not move with width at all. Above half
 *    the height it is a lens whose taper grows with the radius and is
 *    bounded only by half the element's WIDTH: at 999px on a 200px-wide
 *    bar the cap runs 78-86px whatever the height. A square element is the
 *    one exception and is immune, because its two axes clamp to the same
 *    number - which is why the 8x8 trait-pip dot was always a correct
 *    circle and hid the defect on every screen that shows a creature card.
 *
 *    SO THERE IS NO PILL TOKEN. Each pill-shaped element carries a literal
 *    radius of half its own measured height, rounded down, written beside
 *    that measurement. Tokens.uss's radius block says why, and why rounding
 *    DOWN is the safe direction.
 *
 * FONTS. Baloo 2 (display) and Nunito (body, and every numeral), both SIL
 * OFL 1.1, under client/Assets/UI/Fonts with their licence.
 *
 * BOUND DIRECTLY, NOT THROUGH A TOKEN, and that is deliberate. Tokens.uss
 * holds values; a font is an asset reference, and threading one through a
 * custom property makes the indirection load-bearing for something that has
 * exactly two values and changes roughly never. The type SCALE is tokenised
 * because it is arithmetic; the FACE is not because it is an asset.
 *
 * BALOO 2 SETS NO NUMBERS, and that is measured rather than preferred. Its
 * digits advance 0-9 as 54, 34.5625, 46.70313, 46.34375, 53.01563, 47.34375,
 * 50.04688, 42.84375, 50.57813, 50.125 - ten distinct widths, so any counter
 * set in it jitters as it ticks. bible 10.6 prescribes the remedy for this
 * exact case: keep the display face for titles and CTAs, and pair a tabular
 * face for numerals only. Nunito Bold's ten are identical, so it is the face
 * every numeral is set in: Baloo 2 is confined to .t-screen-title, .t-section
 * and .btn-primary, and .t-hero and .t-num take Nunito.
 *
 * bible 10.6's two hard requirements are now both gated: tabular figures by
 * TypographyTests, and the 11px numeral floor by verify-uss-tokens.sh via the
 * .t-num marker below. Neither was checkable before this phase.
 */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- surfaces */

.shell-root {
    background-color: var(--paper);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- text */

Label, Button, TextField {
    -unity-font-definition: url("project:///Assets/UI/Fonts/Nunito-Bold%20SDF.asset");
}

/* Baloo 2 carries the display voice, and nothing that shows a number. The
 * header explains the measurement that put .t-hero on the other side of this
 * line despite it being the largest type in the app. */
.t-screen-title, .t-section, .btn-primary > Label, .btn-primary {
    -unity-font-definition: url("project:///Assets/UI/Fonts/Baloo2-Bold%20SDF.asset");
}

/* The marker verify-uss-tokens.sh enforces the 11px floor against. Any label
 * showing a number a decision depends on carries it. */
.t-num {
    -unity-font-definition: url("project:///Assets/UI/Fonts/Nunito-Bold%20SDF.asset");
    font-size: var(--text-secondary);
}

/* NO -unity-font-style: bold HERE, DELIBERATELY. The rule above binds every
 * Label to Nunito-Bold SDF, which IS the bold face. Asking for bold on top of
 * it does not select a heavier typeface - the asset's m_FontWeightTable is
 * empty, so TextCore has nothing to swap to and falls back to synthetic
 * emboldening, a 0.75 weight dilation over a face that is already 700. That
 * thickens strokes and closes counters worst at --text-secondary (11px) and
 * --text-micro (10px), which are exactly the sizes bible 10.6's "1/l/I and
 * 0/O must be unambiguous" is about. Weight comes from the face, not from a
 * style flag. */
Label {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
}

.t-screen-title {
    font-size: var(--text-screen-title);
    color: var(--ink);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

.t-section {
    font-size: var(--text-section);
    color: var(--ink);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

.t-hero {
    font-size: var(--text-hero);
    color: var(--ink);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

.t-card-title {
    font-size: var(--text-card-title);
    color: var(--ink);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

.t-secondary {
    font-size: var(--text-secondary);
    color: var(--mute);
}

/* Uppercase micro label - handoff: 9-10px, 800, letter-spacing .1em, mute. */
.t-micro {
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    color: var(--mute);
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

.t-danger  { color: var(--coral-text); }
.t-success { color: var(--green-text); }
.t-warn    { color: var(--amber-text); }
.t-violet  { color: var(--violet-text); }

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- surfaces */

.card {
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
}

.row {
    background-color: var(--surface-sunk);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding: var(--space-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

/* The predicted-hybrid panel, and the one panel in the set whose fill is a
 * gradient rather than a flat tint - `Splice Chamber.dc.html` is explicit
 * that this surface is different from the cards above and below it. USS has
 * no gradient (this file's header, item 1), so it is a ramp texture like the
 * CTA's, stretched.
 *
 * THE COLOUR UNDER THE IMAGE IS DELIBERATE AND IS THE OPPOSITE CALL FROM
 * `.btn-primary`, WHICH SETS IT TRANSPARENT. There, `--violet` was already
 * on the element from the `Button` rule and would have tinted the ramp. Here
 * nothing is under it, so the fill costs nothing while the image is present
 * and is what the panel degrades to if the texture ever fails to import -
 * the panel's own deepest tint rather than white. This phase exists partly
 * because a missing asset rendered as nothing and nobody noticed.
 *
 * NOT `.btn-primary--hybrid`, which is what this task's brief named. That
 * would be a modifier on the primary CTA, and nothing in the phase composes
 * one: the ramp belongs to a SectionCard in the splice chamber, which is a
 * panel. Named for what it is.
 *
 * NO CONSUMER IN THE APP AS OF PHASE 9 TASK 16, AND NOT RETIRED - DO NOT
 * REACH FOR IT. The predicted-hybrid panel turned out to be `HeroBand`,
 * measured property by property against `Splice Chamber.dc.html:101`: this
 * class would be carried by a `SectionCard`, which is `.elev-1` (0 2px 8px
 * .06) where the handoff's own surface is `0 4px 16px rgba(63,58,82,.08)` -
 * `.elev-2`, a whole elevation step wrong - and --hybrid-tint-deep is 25 in
 * summed channel distance from the handoff's `#f4f0fb` against the band's
 * 8. `hybrid-ramp.png` has no consumer either, for the same reason.
 * Retiring a Theme class and an asset is a palette-owner call and is not
 * Task 16b's; this line exists so Tasks 17-19 do not compose a surface that
 * was already measured and beaten. */
.panel-hybrid {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/hybrid-ramp.png");
    -unity-background-scale-mode: stretch-to-fill;
    background-color: var(--hybrid-tint-deep);
}

.panel-violet { background-color: var(--violet-tint); border-radius: var(--radius-row); padding: var(--space-3); }
.panel-teal   { background-color: var(--teal-tint);   border-radius: var(--radius-row); padding: var(--space-3); }
.panel-green  { background-color: var(--green-tint);  border-radius: var(--radius-row); padding: var(--space-3); }
.panel-amber  { background-color: var(--amber-tint);  border-radius: var(--radius-row); padding: var(--space-3); }
.panel-coral  { background-color: var(--coral-tint);  border-radius: var(--radius-row); padding: var(--space-3); }

.divider {
    height: 1px;
    background-color: var(--divider);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------- elevation */

/* WRAP a surface in one of these; do not put them ON the surface. See note 2
 * in this file's header for why UI Toolkit leaves no other option, and
 * ScreenFixtures' Primitives fixture for the arrangement Tasks 8-12 copy:
 *
 *     <ui:VisualElement class="elev-1">
 *         <ui:VisualElement class="card"> ... </ui:VisualElement>
 *     </ui:VisualElement>
 *
 * The slice scale is padding/10 and so differs per class - one value cannot
 * serve both spreads. It carries a unit because a bare 0.5 imports as
 * "warning: Expected (<length>) but found '0.5'", and this phase exists
 * partly because a USS diagnostic went unread for seven phases. */
.elev-1, .elev-2 {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/shadow-card.png");
    -unity-slice-left: 20; -unity-slice-right: 20;
    -unity-slice-top: 20; -unity-slice-bottom: 20;
}
/* THE NEGATIVE MARGIN IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE ARRANGEMENT, AND IT WAS
 * MISSING FOR TWO TASKS. Task 3's own brief asked for the shadow as "a
 * sibling element, not a style ... with a negative margin". The sibling was
 * correctly rejected - UI Toolkit clips a background image to the element's
 * own box, so a shadow has to be drawn by something LARGER than the surface,
 * which is this wrapper - but the negative margin went with it, and that was
 * the half that kept the surface on the gutter.
 *
 * Without it the padding insets the SURFACE rather than expanding the shadow
 * outward, so an elevated card sits --elev-1-spread in from wherever an
 * unelevated sibling sits. Measured on the Task 9 corpus: on
 * FounderNamingView, the SectionCard's white edge was at x=18 and the CTA
 * button directly beneath it at x=12 - 6px out of line, on the same screen,
 * visible. .elev-2 would have been 12px out.
 *
 * HORIZONTAL ONLY, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A HALF-FIX. Three
 * reasons, in order of weight:
 *
 *   1. THE SHADOW IS ASYMMETRIC VERTICALLY, BY DESIGN. Measured on
 *      LineageView between two stacked SectionCards, column x=215: the ink
 *      below a card's bottom edge runs 6px deep and reaches 4/255, and above
 *      the next card's top edge it runs 2px and reaches 1/255. That fall is
 *      the whole point of a drop shadow and it is drawn INSIDE this padding.
 *      Cancelling the padding vertically would put the next card's white edge
 *      inside the previous card's shadow.
 *   2. NOTHING IS WRONG VERTICALLY. Two stacked cards measure 20px white edge
 *      to white edge - 6 + --space-2 + 6 - which is what SectionCard.uss says
 *      it tuned for. A negative top and bottom margin makes that 8px.
 *   3. HOLDING 20px WITH A VERTICAL NEGATIVE MARGIN would mean re-tuning
 *      `.section-card`'s margin-bottom from 8px to 20px, and 20 is not on the
 *      spacing scale - 12 and 24 sit either side. That is a one-use value
 *      invented to keep a gap that is already right.
 *
 * Horizontally the shadow is symmetric and 5-6px wide, and moving it off the
 * surface and into the gutter is exactly what a drop shadow should do. It
 * now draws OUTSIDE the wrapper's layout box; verified against the leftmost
 * card on every capture that has one, which is inside the scaffold's
 * ScrollView and is not clipped by it.
 *
 * A LITERAL, BECAUSE USS HAS NO calc() HERE - TRIED, MEASURED, REVERTED.
 * `calc(var(--elev-1-spread) * -1)` is what this wants to say, and on
 * 6000.6.0f1 it imports as
 *
 *     warning: Unknown function 'calc' in declaration 'margin-left: calc'
 *     warning: Expected (<length-percentage> | auto) but found 'calc(...)'
 *
 * and the declaration is DROPPED. Nothing else happens: the sheet still
 * compiles, check-stylesheets.sh stays green, every test passes, and the
 * capture came back byte-identical to the run before it - sixteen of
 * sixteen. That is this file's own history repeating (Tokens.uss's four
 * unread LineBreakUnexpected warnings), so it is recorded rather than
 * silently worked around.
 *
 * SO THE TWO NUMBERS BELOW MUST EQUAL --elev-1-spread AND --elev-2-spread
 * NEGATED, AND NOTHING IN USS CAN ENFORCE THAT. verify-uss-tokens.sh check 4
 * does: it reads both spreads out of Tokens.uss and reddens if either margin
 * here stops being the negation. Change a spread, change these, or the gate
 * says so. */
.elev-1 {
    padding: var(--elev-1-spread);
    margin-left: -6px;
    margin-right: -6px;
    -unity-slice-scale: 0.6px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--elev-1-tint);
}
.elev-2 {
    padding: var(--elev-2-spread);
    margin-left: -12px;
    margin-right: -12px;
    -unity-slice-scale: 1.2px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--elev-2-tint);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- buttons */

/* Unity's runtime theme gives Button its own border, margin and tinted
 * background. Every one of those is overridden here rather than per screen,
 * because a screen that forgets to do it looks like a different app. */
Button {
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    font-size: var(--text-cta);
    color: var(--surface);
    background-color: var(--violet);
    background-image: none;
    border-left-width: 0;
    border-right-width: 0;
    border-top-width: 0;
    border-bottom-width: 3px;
    border-bottom-color: var(--violet-shadow);
    border-radius: var(--radius-cta);
    padding-top: 12px;
    padding-bottom: 12px;
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    padding-right: var(--space-4);
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-right: 0;
    transition-property: translate, border-bottom-width, background-color;
    transition-duration: var(--motion-press);
}

/* bible 10.6's physical press. See note 3 in this file's header. */
Button:active {
    translate: 0 2px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    background-color: var(--violet-deep);
}

Button:disabled {
    background-color: var(--hairline);
    color: var(--mute-soft);
    border-bottom-color: var(--hairline);
}

/* Primary: the handoff's CTA gradient, as the ramp texture. See note 1 in
 * this file's header. `background-color` goes transparent because a colour
 * under a `background-image` is what the image is composited over, and
 * leaving `--violet` there tints the ramp. */
/* THE PRIMARY CTA IS A NINE-SLICE, NOT A RAMP, as of Phase 10 Task 1.1.
 * ns-btn-primary.png carries the lit crown, the face and the 3px foot in one
 * picture (generate-textures.py has the geometry), so the Button rule's own
 * bottom border is switched OFF here in both states - otherwise the foot
 * would be drawn twice. The press keeps bible 10.6's motion: the sprite
 * swaps to the pressed one (a 1px foot, darker face) as the element drops
 * 2px, which is the same silhouette change the border used to make.
 * Slice scale 0.5 because the sprite is authored at 2x; the 40px border is
 * the one ArtImportSettings.cs re-asserts. */
.btn-primary {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/ns-btn-primary.png");
    -unity-slice-left: 40; -unity-slice-right: 40;
    -unity-slice-top: 40; -unity-slice-bottom: 40;
    -unity-slice-scale: 0.5px;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    border-bottom-width: 0;
}
.btn-primary:active {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/ns-btn-primary-pressed.png");
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    border-bottom-width: 0;
}

/* The reward button - claim a gift, collect a milestone. Gold is reserved
 * for rewards (bible 10.1 as amended), so this is the ONLY gold button. */
.btn-reward {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/ns-btn-reward.png");
    -unity-slice-left: 40; -unity-slice-right: 40;
    -unity-slice-top: 40; -unity-slice-bottom: 40;
    -unity-slice-scale: 0.5px;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    border-bottom-width: 0;
    color: var(--reward-text);
}
.btn-reward:active {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/ns-btn-reward-pressed.png");
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    border-bottom-width: 0;
}

/* THE DARK FRAME. Battle readouts, the HUD, a portrait stage's surround:
 * slate with a brass edge, as a nine-slice because USS cannot draw two
 * borders. Labels inside it read in --paper, set by the rule below rather
 * than per screen. */
.panel-deep {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/ns-panel-deep.png");
    -unity-slice-left: 48; -unity-slice-right: 48;
    -unity-slice-top: 48; -unity-slice-bottom: 48;
    -unity-slice-scale: 0.5px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: var(--space-4);
}
.panel-deep Label { color: var(--paper); }

/* Secondary: violet on tint, per the handoff's secondary button. */
.btn-secondary {
    background-color: var(--violet-tint);
    color: var(--violet-text);
    border-bottom-color: var(--violet-edge);
}
.btn-secondary:active { background-color: var(--violet-pressed); }

/* Success and danger CTAs - green-deep and coral-deep per the token table. */
.btn-success { background-color: var(--green); border-bottom-color: var(--green-deep); }
.btn-danger  { background-color: var(--coral); border-bottom-color: var(--coral-deep); }

/* A text-only action: no fill, no drop edge. Used for "skip" and the like. */
.btn-quiet {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    color: var(--mute);
    border-bottom-width: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-body);
}
.btn-quiet:active { translate: 0 0; color: var(--violet-text); }

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ text fields */

TextField {
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
}

TextField > * {
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--hairline);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--text-card-title);
    padding: 10px;
}

TextField:focus > * {
    border-color: var(--ring);
    border-width: 2px;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ chips */

/* THE HEIGHT IS THE CHIP'S, NOT THE LABEL'S, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE FIX.
 * Phase 9 Task 16b. Measured off the capture corpus rather than reasoned:
 *
 *   - `.chip` WORN BY A LABEL rendered 22px - this rule's old 4+4 of
 *     vertical padding around a 14px line box. `InheritanceBar`'s DOM/REC
 *     tag (SpliceChamberView.png, y=665..686) and `CampaignSelectView`'s
 *     Cleared chip (CampaignSelectView.png, y=133..154) are both that shape
 *     and both measured exactly 22.
 *   - `.chip` WORN BY A CONTAINER with a Label inside it rendered 37-38:
 *     `GenChip` 37 (RosterView.png, y=109..145), `TraitChip` 38
 *     (y=300..337). Same 8px of padding, plus TraitChip's 1px border a
 *     side - so the INNER Label was measuring 28-29, which is TWO line
 *     boxes, where the identical Label as the chip itself measures one.
 *
 * SO IT WAS NEVER THE FONT'S LINE METRICS. Nunito-Bold SDF is m_LineHeight
 * 122.76 on m_PointSize 90 = 1.36em: 13.6px at --text-micro, 15.0 at
 * --text-secondary, and `.pill`'s own note below measured the same 1.36
 * independently at --text-body (25px = 8 + 16.4). Changing the asset's
 * metrics would move every Label in the application to fix a number that is
 * already right. WHY a child Label measures two line boxes where a leaf
 * measures one is NOT instrumented here and is not claimed; what is
 * measured is that a height on the chip makes both shapes one box.
 *
 * *** TASK 20 INSTRUMENTED IT, AND IT IS NOT TWO LINE BOXES. ***
 * `LabelBoxProbe` (implementation/scripts/probe-label-box.sh) reads
 * resolvedStyle off a real panel: Unity's DEFAULT RUNTIME THEME gives every
 * Label padding 4/4 and margin 4/2. So at --text-micro the inner Label's
 * border box is 4 + 14 + 4 = 22 and its MARGIN box is 4 + 22 + 2 = 28. That
 * is one 14px line box inside 14px of fixed chrome - and 28 is what the
 * measurements above read as "TWO line boxes", because 2 x 13.6 = 27.2 sits
 * 0.8px away from it.
 *
 * IT REPRODUCES BOTH NUMBERS ABOVE EXACTLY. A Label WEARING `.chip` gets the
 * chip's own 4+4 around its 14px line box = 22. A CONTAINER wearing `.chip`
 * gets 4+4 around the inner Label's 28px margin box = 36, plus TraitChip's
 * 1px border a side = 38. Being a constant, the chrome also cannot scale
 * with font-size, which is the non-linearity Task 18 recorded at StatCell.
 *
 * THE RULE ABOVE IS UNAFFECTED AND STILL RIGHT - a height on the box wins
 * whatever the child's chrome does. The cheaper general fix, if one is ever
 * wanted, is zeroing the Label's own padding and margin: the probe shows
 * that takes `TraitPip` from 34px to exactly `TraitChip`'s 20. NOT DONE
 * HERE - it would move every Label in the application, which is the same
 * objection this note already makes about the font asset's metrics.
 *
 * THE VERTICAL PADDING IS GONE RATHER THAN LEFT BESIDE THE HEIGHT. `height`
 * in UI Toolkit is border-box - Yoga sizes padding and border INSIDE it -
 * so with a height those two declarations could only shrink the content box
 * the label is centred in, which is a declaration that no longer decides
 * anything. The horizontal padding still does, and stays.
 *
 * `-unity-text-align` IS INHERITED, which is what puts a CHILD label on the
 * chip's centre line as well as a label that IS the chip - the same
 * inheritance TraitChip.uss and GenChip.uss already cancel
 * `-unity-font-style: bold` through, one level down. `justify-content`
 * centres the child ELEMENT, because a Label that still measures taller
 * than the chip has to straddle the middle rather than hang below it. */
.chip {
    background-color: var(--violet-tint);
    color: var(--violet-text);
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    height: var(--chip-height);
    justify-content: center;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    padding-left: var(--space-2);
    padding-right: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

/* Half of a MEASURED 25px, rounded down - see Theme.uss header item 4 and
 * Tokens.uss's radius block for why this is a literal and not a token, and
 * why down is the safe direction to round. Measured under a real panel on a
 * `Label.pill` at --text-body with this rule's own 4px vertical padding.
 * NOT VERIFIED IN THE CORPUS: nothing in client/ carries `.pill` today, so
 * no capture renders it and this number is a forward commitment. If a screen
 * gives it a different font-size or padding, re-measure - the height and
 * this radius are coupled.
 *
 * AND `.chip` SETS A HEIGHT NOW, SO A PILL THAT IS ALSO A CHIP IS 20px
 * WHATEVER THIS PADDING SAYS - --chip-height is border-box and wins over
 * four paddings that only move the content box inside it. At 20 the
 * semicircle is 10, not 12. Compose the two and re-measure; that is the
 * coupling the paragraph above means, reached from the other side. */
.pill {
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding-top: var(--space-1);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-1);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    padding-right: var(--space-3);
}

/* THE HANDOFF'S HEADER AFFORDANCE: a white tile at the header's left edge,
 * lifted off the paper rather than sunk into it. Every pushed screen in the
 * bundle wears one - the back chevron on Splice Chamber, the pause control on
 * Wave Defense - at `background: #ffffff; box-shadow: 0 1px 3px
 * rgba(63,58,82,.1)`.
 *
 * THE SHADOW IS A 1px --hairline BORDER, AND THAT IS FORCED. USS has no
 * box-shadow (this file's header, item 2) and the nine-slice answer does not
 * reach here: `.elev-1` is a WRAPPER - padding plus the sprite, with the
 * surface as a child inside it - and a Button draws its own label and glyph
 * in its own box, so it cannot be the child of a wrapper without ceasing to
 * be the control. Put `.elev-1` on the Button itself instead and the
 * nine-slice stretches its centre region across the interior and paints a
 * grey smudge under the chevron, which this project has shipped twice. A
 * hairline is the honest substitute for a 3px-blur shadow at this size.
 *
 * NOT A PILL, THOUGH THE NAME SAYS SO. The name is fixed by the phase's
 * component contract; the SHAPE is the handoff's, which is a 34px rounded
 * SQUARE at radius 12 (`Splice Chamber.dc.html`) or 11 (`Wave Defense`), not
 * a 17px circle. --radius-tile (11px) sits in the handoff's own 9-14px
 * icon-tile band and is what the back control already carried.
 *
 * Radius, size and padding stay with the element that has them - the
 * scaffold's back Button keeps its own rule. This class is the FILL, so a
 * screen that needs the same white tile for its own control (the pause
 * button, the speed pill) gets it here rather than re-deriving it. */
.pill-back {
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--hairline);
}

/* Selected / unselected option, from the handoff's exact rule. USS has no
 * inset box-shadow, so the 2px ring is a real border and the 1px hairline is
 * the same border at 1px - the layout cost is identical because both states
 * carry a border, which is why the unselected one is specified at all. */
.option {
    background-color: var(--surface-sunk);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding: var(--space-3);
}

.option--selected {
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: var(--ring);
}
